Page 1 of 4

The Baby Bulldogs chase their red ball across the grass until it rolls beside a puppy sitting
alone. The puppy nudges it back without looking up.
Page 2 of 4

One Bulldog invites the puppy to the swing. Another talks so excitedly that the invitation
gets lost beneath a tumble of words.
Page 3 of 4

Luckybug draws a chalk line with a star at each end. The puppy can join by rolling the ball
across—no talking needed.
Page 4 of 4

Soon the playground has one longer team and one very waggy new friend. The Bulldogs
learn that a welcome can be an action, not only a question.
Questions for Baby Bulldogs at the Playground

Read the story together first. Use one question at each page, then the question at the end. Answers are below
the dotted line: fold them under or cover them while reading.
Page 1

Talk together: What might the quiet puppy be feeling?
Page 2
Talk together: Why might the invitation feel difficult to answer?
Page 3
Talk together: How does the chalk game make joining easier?
Page 4
Talk together: What welcoming action worked best?
After reading
Why did the chalk game help the quiet puppy join in?
Answers (fold under before reading)

After reading: The puppy could play without having to speak. Rolling the ball let the puppy answer the welcome
with an action.

